e need to check if it's valid. Let's analyze more thoroughly.\n\nWe have n columns. For each column, we need to cover all its points. Sunny lines can cover at most one point per column. So for each column, the number of points covered by sunny lines is at most 1. So the total number of columns that have at least one point covered by a sunny line is at most k (since each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, but a sunny line can cover points in multiple columns (one per column). Actually, a sunny line can intersect many columns, each at most one point. So a single sunny line can cover points in many columns. So the statement \"having k ≥ 4 would force at least four columns to rely on sunny lines for at least one of their points\" is not necessarily true: a single sunny line could cover points in many columns, so k=4 could still have only 4 sunny lines, each covering points in many columns. But the argument is that each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, but can cover points in many columns. So the number of columns that have at least one sunny point is at most the total number of points covered by sunny lines, which could be up to sum of points per line. But we need to bound the number of columns that have at least one sunny point? Actually, each sunny line can intersect each column at most once, but can intersect many columns. So with k sunny lines, we could have up to k points per column? No, each column can have at most k sunny points (one per sunny line). So the total number of columns that have at least one sunny point could be as many as n (if each column has at least one sunny point). But we have only k sunny lines, each can intersect each column at most once, but they can intersect all columns. So with k=4, we could have each of the 4 sunny lines intersect all n columns, covering 4 points per column (one per line). So the claim that \"k ≥ 4 would force at least four columns to rely on sunny lines for at least one of their points\"